            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Abundance, divine provision, and the richness of the land, often coupled with honey; alternatively, it symbolizes a diet of pastoral simplicity forced by national judgment and agricultural devastation.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Isaiah 7:15\",\"Isaiah 7:22\",\"Job 20:17\",\"Judges 5:25\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"The true humanity and humble, ordinary upbringing of the incarnate Christ.\",\"basis\":[\"Isaiah 7:15\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Deceptive flattery, hypocrisy, or smooth but dangerous words.\",\"basis\":[\"Psalm 55:21\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"Hospitality and honor toward a guest, sometimes as a precursor to betrayal.\",\"basis\":[\"Judges 5:25\"],\"prominence\":\"rare\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":false,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The symbolic meaning of butter or curds (chemah) heavily depends on the context. In the Immanuel prophecy of Isaiah 7, commentators debate whether the diet of 'curds and honey' represents the abundance of God's blessing or the deprivation of a land reduced to uncultivated pasture by invading armies. In Messianic interpretation, it frequently denotes Christ's incarnation and partaking in ordinary human weakness.\"}",
